---
import SiteShell from "../components/SiteShell.astro";
---

<SiteShell title="The brief">
    <div class="max-w-3xl py-14">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest">The course</p>
        <h1 class="mt-3 font-heading text-5xl">The brief</h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-relaxed">A project on your laptop can depend on you being there to explain it. A public project has to welcome someone on its own.</p>

        <section class="mt-12 border-t border-gray-300 pt-8">
            <h2 class="font-heading text-3xl">Your project</h2>
            <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed">Put a version of your studio work on a public URL. If the work is digital, let people experience it. If it is physical, bring one meaningful part of it into the browser. Either way, document the whole project: the world, the artefact, the decisions, and what you learned by making it.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="mt-9 border-t border-gray-300 pt-8">
            <h2 class="font-heading text-3xl">Our showcase</h2>
            <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed">Build one site together for the class’s work. It should make room for each project, and for the people behind it: what we care about, the choices we made, and the interactions we wanted to try. Everyone contributes through GitHub; the site lives on the public web.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="mt-9 border-t border-gray-300 pt-8">
            <h2 class="font-heading text-3xl">The test</h2>
            <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed">Send someone a link. Give them no introduction. Can they find their way in, use the work, and tell you what stayed with them? The answer is material for the next version.</p>
        </section>
        <a href="/today" class="mt-12 inline-block bg-[#ccff00] px-5 py-3 font-semibold shadow-[3px_3px_0_#202020]">Start with today →</a>
    </div>
</SiteShell>
